Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
we begin the day with a deadly crackdown  in kazakhstan that is looking more and  more like a major strategic victory for  the kremlin tonight russian soldiers are  in the central asian republic allegedly  there as peacekeepers they were invited  this week after protests over high gas  prices low wages and government  corruption turned violent with  kazakhstan's president authorizing  police to shoot without warning  in the largest city almaty demonstrators  set the city hall on fire and seize the  airport dozens of anti-government  protesters have died hundreds more are  injured police say that 13 officers were  killed in what has quickly become the  biggest crisis in kazakhstan since it  gained independence 30 years ago  today the government said for the most  part order has been restored now  president tokayev is blaming the unrest  on what he calls bandits terrorists and  militants  terrorists continue to damage state and  private property and use weapons on  citizens  i have ordered law enforcement agencies  and the army to shoot to kill  without warning  we are dealing with armed and trained  bandits both local and foreign  i repeat their bandits and terrorists  therefore they need to be destroyed  i would like to express my special  gratitude to president vladimir putin of  russia  he reacted very promptly and most  importantly in a friendly manner to my  appeal  well for years kazakhstan has been  walking a geopolitical tightrope between  the east the west and russia  kazakhstan's location makes it a bridge  between those fast-growing markets of  china and south asia  as well as russia and europe as a major  oil and gas exporter kazakhstan has  enjoyed strong economic ties with the  united states and the european union  could that now be in jeopardy with the  russian troops in the country is  kazakhstan now beholden  to the kremlin  well my first guest tonight is ben ares  he's a long time correspondent in moscow  and founder of the media company bne  intel news he joins me tonight from  right here in berlin ben it's good to  have you on the program let me ask you  has  this equation has it changed is  kazakhstan now forever in debt to the  kremlin for coming to the rescue this  week  um yes and no uh i mean to take you  through i mean it's been an  extraordinary week the events in  kazakhstan are unfolded i'm president  right the whole thing came out of  nowhere at the weekend last week uh when  there were protests in the the western  regions over these these price hikes of  fuel for cars but that spread to the  rest of the country by tuesday  and then the government quickly came in  and addressed the economic demands of  the protesters and reintroduced price  subsidies but that didn't work by by  wednesday the protests morphed into a  sort of general anti-government movement  and that point then uh  the president up the stakes and he went  from calling the the protesters  demonstrators to calling them hooligans  and said what they were doing is illegal  and he took the the riot troops went out  to the streets and tried to take the  situation back in control but by the end  of wednesday it was clear that the  government was losing control and that  that was the point that they they turned  to russia or rather to the csto this uh  joint cooperation and security  organization that they set up the sort  of eurasian equivalent of nato and asked  for help um at which point the russians  agreed to send along with the other five  members four members  agreed to send troops which arriving  this morning  and what we're seeing now is the with  the arrival of the troops the situation  has changed again the government seems  to be back in control but now there's  the follow through the cleanup operation  where they're cracking down where  they've ordered shoot to kill in order  to bring the protest to a quick end and  let me ask you as far as vladimir putin  is concerned do you think he is  we know that he can be an opportunist if  he's seizing on the unrest  and sending his troops in to expand his  influence in this country  now it's more to do with the last thing  the kremlin wants to see is yet another  color revolution there was one a year  ago in kyrgyzstan next door um where the  the alliances the friends i mean you  have to understand that russia is is  deeply invested into kazakhstan that it  has um business um ties with it the  russian companies are invested in there  in oil business and the pipelines run  out kazakhstan through russia which  earns money from that and buys the oil  and so  all said and done  putin does not want to see another color  revolution a sort of democratic  government put in here and takayev is a  partner their longtime partner and that  he um he  his concern is to rescue  have to keep stability to keep the  relations they already have rather than  territory expansion and nor will they  leave troops there i think once uh once  the order is restored now you've said  you know the events happened so quickly  this week um do you think all of this  caught both  you know the u.s nato as well as russia  off guard i mean it seems you know you  named kyrgyzstan there's also been  ukraine there's been belarus it seems  they have been sucking all of the oxygen  for the past months  yeah i mean of course everybody was by  surprise i mean this came out of nowhere  i mean the the the march is the protest  that we saw in rmit  on tuesday are unprecedented tens of  thousands of people and there's been  low-level protests been going on for  years particularly when the elections  when the old president nazarbayev left  and takaya took over in 2019 there were  protests but they quickly smothered and  the people are arrested and given  administrative 15 days in jail and let  out again but this this is off the scale  and it came from nowhere and so  everybody scrambled in order to try and  catch up  particularly the government trying to  regain control and the kremlin when  asked acted very quickly because  likewise they don't want to see another  after ukraine particularly after belarus  russia which is a huge pain in the neck  for russia they don't want to see  another long protracted demonstration  pro-democracy democracy demonstration  again  journal it's been ours giving us  valuable context of the situation in  kazakhstan tonight ben we appreciate  your time and your insight thank you  nato leaders met today to discuss the  situation as we were discussing on  ukraine's border with russia where  moscow is massing thousands of troops  officials and capitals from washington  to berlin and kiev fear that russian  president vladimir putin could be  preparing an invasion after ukraine  kazakhstan joins the list of countries  where putin is trying to flex his  military muscles earlier today our  brussels correspondent terry schultz  tried to pick the brains of native  secretary general ian stoltenberg she  asked him how he thinks russia's  intervention in kazakhstan fits into the  geopolitical puzzle that putin is  piecing together in the kremlin here's  what he said  i will not speculate  on  in what way the developments in  in in kazakhstan will impact  calculations by  or in moscow i think the main message on  on kazakhstan is that violence must stop  we have seen the loss of lives and  and human rights must be respected and  that also  includes freedom of expression and  peaceful demonstration  all right and my colleague now terry  schultz she is in brussels  terry it's good to have you on the show  again and happy new year to you let me  ask you  stoltenberg  it's refusing to stick his head out of  the window it seems with with his answer  is there a  kazakh connection here  i wasn't really surprised brent that uh  stoltenberg wouldn't take the bait on  offering me some free analysis there it  is press conference because there really  isn't a direct connection between nato  and kazakhstan um i would say there's  sort of a cordial relationship but but  nothing too involved and so nato really  won't want to be getting involved  in commenting on kazakhstan i think what  everyone's looking at now is what this  will do to the kremlin's behavior  whether president putin will be  distracted now by what's happening in  kazakhstan a country that has always  been quite stable and following a  positive line toward russia whether this  will distract him from what he's doing  in ukraine and then that of course could  have an impact on nato did you get the  impression that nato was maybe called  off guard by what has happened so  quickly in kazakhstan  i think certainly as ben eris explained  everyone was caught off guard by how  quickly the  developments in kazakhstan occurred  anyone who's looked at this country or  countries in this region knows that  there is a lot of resentment building up  for for decades among some segments of  the population so it's not surprising  that the resentment was there but that  it gained so much power in such a short  time i think did take everyone by  surprise and probably president putin as  well and again that's what everyone  wants to know what will president putin  do will he devote more of his resources  to helping settle the ground in  kazakhstan and what will that mean for  his continuing build up on the border  with ukraine yeah and you know as i  mentioned earlier today's meeting was  called to discuss the situation with  ukraine here's a little bit more of what  the nato secretary general had to say  nato will engage in dialogue with russia  in good faith  and on substance  but we must also be prepared  for the possibility  that diplomacy will fail  so today ministers  stressed that any further aggression  against ukraine would have significant  consequences and carry a heavy price for  russia  now terry if i'm hearing that right  there seems to be some doubt coming from  stoughton berg that this crisis can be  solved through diplomacy is he preparing  the ground for a possible  failure been some developments in recent  days most notably the the plan for us  and russia uh the u.s and russia to have  their meeting on monday and then for  russia to actually come to nato for  discussions next wednesday that's a big  deal because nato's been asking for  these consultations for more than 18  months and russia has always refused now  it will come to the table with ukraine  as the central topic for those talks but  certainly no one believes that diplomacy  is going to have an easy path and nato's  military planners were given orders back  in december formally to start preparing  military scenarios for the possibility  that russia does go ahead militarily and  and makes further  further inroads into ukraine or  certainly threatens nato allies and  brent tonight there's also a development  uh a report coming out of the united  states nbc news we must give credit  there that says the u.s may be  considering offering to russia as part  of its uh its hopes for reciprocity on  backing down on the border with ukraine  that u.s troops may be reduced in europe  and that's something that i've already  been speaking with people about that is  certainly not coming as any reassuring  sign to european allies here  yeah that that is a new development to  any talk about reducing the numbers of  u.s troops in europe  let's stick with the topic of the number  of troops and the russian buildup along  its border with ukraine we know that  this buildup continues so tonight based  on what you're reporting how real then  is the danger of a russian invasion  i think that the the possibility of a  russian invasion hasn't changed so much  but the capability to do so continues to  grow tonight secretary general  stoltenberg and other uh officials i  spoke to off the record today said that  it very definitely continues that russia  has not paused despite moves toward  diplomatic discussion with the u.s and  with nato it has continued to build up  in a significant way its capabilities  for an invasion should president putin  decide to do so at the same time  everyone says they really don't know if  those are the kremlin's plans yeah and  if it is true that the u.s is talking  about reducing its troop numbers in  europe that will of course catch nato  leaders completely off guard blindside  that's not going to help unity within  nato dw's terry schultz with the ladies  tonight in brussels as always terry  thank you  you

